        <fr:tree toc="true" numbered="false" show-heading="true" show-metadata="true" expanded="false" root="false" xmlns:fr="http://www.jonmsterling.com/jms-005P.xml">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:anchor>2304</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">hodge-theory-iii-10.4.4</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>hodge-theory-iii-10.4.4.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:number>10.4.4</fr:number>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p>Let us translate this theorem.
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> be as in <fr:ref addr="hodge-theory-iii-10.4.2" href="hodge-theory-iii-10.4.2.xml" number="10.4.2" /> (for example, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S=\mathbb {P}^2(\mathbb {C})]]></fr:tex>), and let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta \in \operatorname {H}^0(U,\Omega _U^2)]]></fr:tex> be a double integral and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c\in \operatorname {H}_2^{\log }(U,\mathbb {Z})]]></fr:tex> a cycle.
    By definition,
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[       \langle \beta ,c\rangle        = \int _c\beta      ]]></fr:tex>
    is a residue of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta ]]></fr:tex>.
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A=\operatorname {H}_\mathrm {m}^1(C)(1)/\operatorname {Pic}^0(S)]]></fr:tex> (an extension of an abelian variety by a torus).
    The 2-form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta ]]></fr:tex> defines a cohomology class <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {cl}(\beta )\in \operatorname {H}_\mathrm {DR}^2(U)]]></fr:tex> and induces a linear form <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta ']]></fr:tex> on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {H}_{\mathrm {DR},2}^{\log }(U)=T_\mathrm {DR}(A)]]></fr:tex>.
    
    The map <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta \mapsto \beta ']]></fr:tex> is rational.
    The class <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> defines <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c'\in  T_\mathbb {Z}(A)\otimes \mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex>, and we have that

    <fr:tree toc="true" numbered="false" show-heading="true" show-metadata="false" expanded="true" root="false" xmlns:fr="http://www.jonmsterling.com/jms-005P.xml"><fr:frontmatter><fr:anchor>2280</fr:anchor><fr:addr type="user">hodge-theory-iii-10.4.4.1</fr:addr><fr:route>hodge-theory-iii-10.4.4.1.xml</fr:route><fr:taxon>Equation</fr:taxon><fr:authors /><fr:number>10.4.4.1</fr:number></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[         \langle \beta ,c\rangle          = 2\pi  i\langle \beta ',c'\rangle .       \tag{10.4.4.1}       ]]></fr:tex></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter /></fr:tree>

    We can represent <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c']]></fr:tex> by a cycle on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex>, and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\beta ']]></fr:tex> as a simple integral on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
          <fr:backmatter />
